  "question": {
    "text": "The figure shows a square loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math> of side <math><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> which is connected to a network of resistances. The whole setup is moving towards the right with a constant speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> . At some instant, a part of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math> is in a uniform magnetic field of&nbsp;<math><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math>&nbsp;perpendicular to the plane of the loop. If the resistance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math> is <math><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>,</mo></math> the current in the loop at that instant will be close to:<br /><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/2b456b23-cb2d-4c38-abba-9dfb52cac127-image.png\" style=\"width: 419px; height: 175px;\" />",
    "image": null
  },
